{"t":"evt","chat":"m2resume","seq":230,"data":{"text":"1. Stick to a consistent sleep schedule by going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, even on weekends, to regulate your body's internal clock.\n\n2. Develop a relaxing bedtime ritual such as reading, gentle stretching, or a warm bath to signal to your brain that it is time to wind down.\n\n3. Transform your bedroom into a sleep sanctuary by keeping it cool, dark, quiet, and free of clutter, with a comfortable mattress and pillows that match your sleeping style.\n\n4. Power down screens and avoid blue light from phones, tablets, and computers at least sixty minutes before bedtime to protect your natural melatonin production.\n\n5. Cut off caffeine, nicotine, and other stimulants by early afternoon so they do not interfere with your ability to fall asleep at night.\n\n6. Incorporate regular physical activity into your day, but finish intense workouts at least three hours before bed to give your body time to cool down.\n\n7. Watch what you eat and drink in the evening by avoiding heavy, spicy, or large meals and limiting liquids close to bedtime to reduce disruptions.\n\n8. If you lie awake for more than twenty minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet in dim light until you feel drowsy, then return to bed to preserve the bed-sleep connection.\n\n9. Ease a racing mind before sleep with journaling, deep breathing, or mindfulness meditation to lower mental arousal and invite rest.\n\n10. Use your bed almost exclusively for sleep and intimacy so your brain forms a strong association between the bed and rest rather than wakeful activities.","stream_type":"message"}}
{"t":"evt","chat":"m2resume","seq":230,"data":{"text":"1. Stick to a consistent sleep schedule by going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, even on weekends, to regulate your body's internal clock.\n\n2. Develop a relaxing bedtime ritual such as reading, gentle stretching, or a warm bath to signal to your brain that it is time to wind down.\n\n3. Transform your bedroom into a sleep sanctuary by keeping it cool, dark, quiet, and free of clutter, with a comfortable mattress and pillows that match your sleeping style.\n\n4. Power down screens and avoid blue light from phones, tablets, and computers at least sixty minutes before bedtime to protect your natural melatonin production.\n\n5. Cut off caffeine, nicotine, and other stimulants by early afternoon so they do not interfere with your ability to fall asleep at night.\n\n6. Incorporate regular physical activity into your day, but finish intense workouts at least three hours before bed to give your body time to cool down.\n\n7. Watch what you eat and drink in the evening by avoiding heavy, spicy, or large meals and limiting liquids close to bedtime to reduce disruptions.\n\n8. If you lie awake for more than twenty minutes, get out of bed and do something quiet in dim light until you feel drowsy, then return to bed to preserve the bed-sleep connection.\n\n9. Ease a racing mind before sleep with journaling, deep breathing, or mindfulness meditation to lower mental arousal and invite rest.\n\n10. Use your bed almost exclusively for sleep and intimacy so your brain forms a strong association between the bed and rest rather than wakeful activities.","stream_type":"message"}}
